THE CDEF FILTER GAP WAS TWO THINGS, AND NEITHER WAS A STALL: **40 % OF THE
CALLS HAD NO VECTOR ARM AT ALL ON x86**, and the arm that existed did C's i16
arithmetic in i32 lanes. Fixing both takes the CDEF filter kernels from
**8.85x C's instructions to 3.03x** on photo_cid 512² p6 (8.04x -> 3.09x on the
screenshot), the whole frame from 2.289x to **2.207x**, and buys
**1.043x / 1.044x WALL CLOCK on the two photos at p6** (1.019x screenshot,
1.051x gradient), byte-identical everywhere.

**The single biggest fact, and it was not in the ranking:** of the 26,816
`cdef_filter_block` calls on that cell, **10,752 (40 %) landed on
`cdef_filter_block_core`, the SCALAR reference**, because
`cdef_filter_block_impl_v3` took the vector path only for `cols == 8` and fell
back for BLOCK_4X8 / BLOCK_4X4. Those 10,752 calls cost **62.7 M of the
88.7 M** CDEF filter instructions — 71 % of the item — against C's
`svt_cdef_filter_block_4xn_8_avx2` at 2.76 M. **22.7x on 40 % of the calls.**
aarch64 did NOT have this hole (its `cdef_filter_cols4_neon` arm already
existed), so it is a pure x86 finding and nothing in the aarch64-side records
could have surfaced it.

